[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Classes in JavaScript (ES6)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"ES6 introduced classes to JavaScript, providing a more structured and familiar syntax for object-oriented programming. While JavaScript's prototype-based inheritance remains under the hood, classes offer a cleaner and more readable way to define and create objects."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining a Class"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A class is defined using the 'class' keyword, followed by the class name. The class body contains the constructor, methods, and properties."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class MyClass {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  greet() {
    console.log('Hello, ' + this.name + '!');
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'MyClass' is defined with a constructor and a 'greet' method."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"The Constructor"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'constructor' method is a special method for creating and initializing an object created with a class. It's automatically called when you create a new instance of the class using the 'new' keyword."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class Person {
  constructor(firstName, lastName) {
    this.firstName = firstName;
    this.lastName = lastName;
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The constructor takes 'firstName' and 'lastName' as arguments and assigns them to the object's properties."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Instances"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create an instance of a class, use the 'new' keyword followed by the class name and any necessary arguments for the constructor."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const person1 = new Person('John', 'Doe');
console.log(person1.firstName); // Output: John
console.log(person1.lastName);  // Output: Doe"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Methods"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Methods are functions defined within a class. They can access and manipulate the object's properties."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"class Rectangle {
  constructor(width, height) {
    this.width = width;
    this.height = height;
  }

  calculateArea() {
    return this.width * this.height;
  }
}

const rect = new Rectangle(5, 10);
console.log(rect.calculateArea()); // Output: 50"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Inheritance"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Classes support inheritance using the 'extends' keyword. A subclass inherits properties and methods from its parent class (superclass)."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  speak() {
    console.log('Generic animal sound');
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name, breed) {
    super(name);
    this.breed = breed;
  }

  speak() {
    console.log('Woof!');
  }
}

const dog = new Dog('Buddy', 'Golden Retriever');
dog.speak(); // Output: Woof!"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'super()' keyword is used to call the constructor of the parent class."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Static Methods"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Static methods are called directly on the class itself, not on instances of the class. They are defined using the 'static' keyword."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"class MathUtils {
  static add(x, y) {
    return x + y;
  }
}

console.log(MathUtils.add(5, 3)); // Output: 8"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Getters and Setters"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Getters and setters allow you to define methods that get or set the value of a property. They are defined using the 'get' and 'set' keywords."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"class Circle {
  constructor(radius) {
    this._radius = radius;
  }

  get radius() {
    return this._radius;
  }

  set radius(value) {
    if (value > 0) {
      this._radius = value;
    } else {
      console.error('Radius must be positive');
    }
  }

  get area() {
    return Math.PI * this._radius * this._radius;
  }
}

const circle = new Circle(5);
console.log(circle.radius); // Output: 5
circle.radius = 10;
console.log(circle.area);   // Output: 314.1592653589793
circle.radius = -1;       // Output: Radius must be positive"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Getters and setters provide a way to control access to object properties and perform validation."}]
